[1mThe bug:[22m The [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m function's API takes [38;5;10mdatalen[0m in [1mbits[22m (not bytes). However, the C code's callers — [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m, [38;5;10mhash_message[0m, and the KAT transcript functions in [38;5;10mPQCgenKAT_sign.c[0m — all pass [1mbyte counts[22m directly to [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m. For example:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10m// C code passes SPX_N=24 (byte count) directly, even though blake512_update expects bits
blakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N);
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This is effectively a quirk of the C reference implementation — it passes byte counts where the API expects bit counts, but since both the signing and verification code do it consistently, the crypto still works internally.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translator noticed that [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m takes bits and "corrected" the callers by multiplying by 8:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
