[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This made the Rust code semantically "correct" but [1mincompatible with the C reference output[22m, since the C code hashes different amounts of data.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe fix:[22m I removed the [38;5;10m* 8[0m multiplications from all [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m call sites in two files:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. [38;5;10mhash_blake.rs[0m — [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m (3 calls) and [38;5;10mhash_message[0m (3 calls)[0m[0m
2. [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m — [38;5;10mkat_tr_init[0m, [38;5;10mkat_tr_absorb_label[0m, [38;5;10mkat_tr_absorb_u64[0m, and [38;5;10mkat_tr_absorb_bytes[0m (8 calls total)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Now the Rust code passes the same byte-count values as the C code, producing identical KAT output.[0m[0m
